#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088899 :tutorial/content "The instantaneous rate of change of a function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$, denoted $f'(a)$, is given by the limit\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}\n$$\n\nThis formula works for a specific point $x=a$. If we want a method that works for a *general* point $x$, then we substitute $a=x$ in the formula and get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nThe function $f'(x)$ is called the **derivative of $f(x)$ with respect to $x$**. It gives the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at a general point with coordinates $(x,f(x))$." :tutorial/id #uuid "098fd6b0-306b-544a-9e7f-b301ff48f525" :tutorial/math-academy-id 327 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088900 :tutorial/content "For a given curve $y=f(x)$, the derivative can be represented using several different notations. They are\n\n$$\nf'(x),\\qquad y'(x), \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nIn words, the term $y'(x)$ is said like\n\n- \"$y$ prime of $x$,\" or\n- \"$y$ prime,\" or sometimes\n- \"$y$ dash.\"\n\nThe term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nis said like \"dee $y$ by dee $x$.\"\n\nThe important thing to remember is that they all represent the same thing, the derivative." :tutorial/id #uuid "cda9f5ac-1ad1-50ce-8bc4-ee5f3be5fd92" :tutorial/math-academy-id 329 :tutorial/title "Alternative Definitions For the Derivative"}]}
